WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.620
A 2000 dollar ambulance bill mostly pays for crew readiness instead of the actual transport. This is First Principles Daily, episode 166. That ride often covers only a few miles. The charge is the expense of keeping a staffed vehicle and a trained crew on standby around the clock. Not the fuel or the chassis time used on that single trip. The billing structure turns readiness itself into the product priced per ride. So a short journey carries a price set by the need to have help available at every hour.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.520
Or whether or not any patient uses it that shift.

00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:38.893
Ambulance services must hold 24-hour coverage to meet response time standards such as 8 minutes

00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:39.833
in urban areas.

00:00:40.413 --> 00:00:44.853
The Massachusetts Ambulance Association states that keeping one unit staffed and ready

00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:52.233
costs about $100 an hour, or $2,400 a day, whether the crew answers 8 calls or 2.

00:00:52.853 --> 00:00:54.953
That daily figure is the rent on a promise.

00:00:54.953 --> 00:00:59.793
The unit can sit quiet for most of a shift and the cost of being there does not shrink.

00:01:00.253 --> 00:01:05.333
Personnel account for roughly 70% of total expenses, according to legal clarity.

00:01:05.853 --> 00:01:11.733
A single ambulance kept ready 24 hours a day, therefore runs about $850,000 a year before

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:12.933
any transport occurs.

00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:18.233
When volume is low, each build trip has to recover a larger share of that fixed daily

00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:18.753
cost.

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:23.373
Medicare reimburses an average of $480 per emergency call.

00:01:23.713 --> 00:01:29.553
Mass health pays around $450 for an advanced life support transport, whose listed rates

00:01:29.553 --> 00:01:32.333
sits between $1900 and $2400.

00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:39.733
Roughly 30% of 911 responses produce no transport at all, yet the readiness expense remains unchanged.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.953
Billing spreads the standby cost across the transports that do occur.

00:01:44.653 --> 00:01:50.113
Mileage fees of $10-20 a mile cover only the marginal fuel and wear on the actual journey.

00:01:50.113 --> 00:01:55.233
The listed price feels normal inside the industry because the service model prices availability

00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:56.973
rather than distance traveled.

00:01:57.493 --> 00:02:02.373
Collection shortfalls widen the gap further, since many build amounts are never recovered

00:02:02.373 --> 00:02:04.153
in full from insurers or patients.

00:02:04.713 --> 00:02:09.873
The structure persists because the dominant expense, continuous crew availability, does

00:02:09.873 --> 00:02:11.873
not shrink when fewer patients are moved.

00:02:12.854 --> 00:02:17.334
The magic wand number is what a thing would cost if you only paid for its raw materials,

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:21.274
as if the atoms could be arranged into the finished object for free.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:25.694
Divide the finished price by that floor and you get the first principles ratio.

00:02:26.294 --> 00:02:29.954
A ratio near one means the thing is already close to its physical limit.

00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:35.474
A large ratio means most of the price is design, process, organization, or rules.

00:02:35.974 --> 00:02:38.854
And that is where a from scratch rethink has room to work.

00:02:39.414 --> 00:02:41.294
The ratio measures where the price sits.

00:02:41.754 --> 00:02:44.034
It is not a verdict on the people who built the thing.

00:02:44.034 --> 00:02:50.154
The ratio applies to things that are designed and made, a part, a product, a plant, a process,

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:52.314
a service built from repeatable steps.

00:02:52.674 --> 00:02:57.554
It says nothing useful about a price set by scarcity of location, a captive audience,

00:02:57.794 --> 00:02:58.254
or a brand.

00:02:58.874 --> 00:03:03.374
When the gap is one of those, the honest move is to say so and stop dressing a market

00:03:03.374 --> 00:03:05.614
or contract story as an engineering one.

00:03:06.154 --> 00:03:10.754
The sources supplied for this episode contain no figures for the raw commodity prices of

00:03:10.754 --> 00:03:16.014
steel, aluminum, medical grade plastics, or electronics that would go into a van chassis

00:03:16.014 --> 00:03:21.634
and its medical module. Without those material quantities and prices, no magic wand

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:26.454
floor can be built, and no first principles ratio can be calculated from the given data.

00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:32.194
I will not invent a materials bill to force the arithmetic. Walk the sum out loud and it stops

00:03:32.194 --> 00:03:36.994
at the first line. No sourced price for the steel, no sourced price for the aluminum,

00:03:36.994 --> 00:03:42.034
no sourced mass of medical grade plastic, no sourced costs for the electronics.

00:03:42.634 --> 00:03:44.634
Add blank lines and the total is blank.

00:03:45.134 --> 00:03:48.554
Divide a listed charge by a blank floor and you do not get a ratio.

00:03:49.074 --> 00:03:51.914
The gap between listed charges and actual trip costs

00:03:51.914 --> 00:03:55.074
therefore sits outside the reach of a material-based ratio.

00:03:56.077 --> 00:03:59.517
The Massachusetts Ambulance Association and Legal Clarity both

00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:04.657
locate the dominant expense in continuous crew wages, training and vehicle availability

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:10.177
capacity, rather than in consumables used during a ride. Fix costs for facilities, insurance

00:04:10.177 --> 00:04:14.977
and equipment add further layers that do not scale with the number of transports. The

00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:19.437
price structure reflects the requirement to purchase coverage on demand. Not a physical

00:04:19.437 --> 00:04:23.217
assembly process whose material content could be isolated and compared.

00:04:23.837 --> 00:04:28.477
The largest single-stage carrying the gap is the continuous payment for trained personnel

00:04:28.477 --> 00:04:34.117
who must remain idle between calls. A secondary stage is the duplication of entire units

00:04:34.117 --> 00:04:38.037
required to guarantee an immediate response when the first unit is already committed.

00:04:38.637 --> 00:04:43.457
When one ambulance leaves on a call, another must already be positioned and staffed, so

00:04:43.457 --> 00:04:48.697
the next emergency does not wait, and communities therefore keep several units on duty at once,

00:04:49.317 --> 00:04:51.877
each accumulating the same daily standby total.

00:04:52.457 --> 00:04:56.697
Those layers are not manufacturing steps that could be streamlined by changing how steel

00:04:56.697 --> 00:04:58.037
or plastics are formed.

00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:03.157
They are scheduling and contractual commitments, and they stay fixed regardless of how many

00:05:03.157 --> 00:05:09.277
miles any one van ultimately travels. The Massachusetts Ambulance Association describes how the cost

00:05:09.277 --> 00:05:14.577
of readiness stays constant regardless of call volume. That constant is the constraint a

00:05:14.577 --> 00:05:20.157
redesign has to meet, not a number that disappears if you reword the invoice. Community

00:05:20.157 --> 00:05:26.237
Paramedicin programs reviewed in a 2024 Springer Nature Study have reduced emergency department

00:05:26.237 --> 00:05:30.317
and transports by 14 to 78% across 11 studies,

00:05:30.677 --> 00:05:33.377
cutting attendance costs by hundreds of dollars per case

00:05:33.377 --> 00:05:35.217
in the higher quality evaluations.

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:38.197
Those programs handle some calls on site

00:05:38.197 --> 00:05:39.797
instead of moving every patient.

00:05:40.417 --> 00:05:43.057
Public utility models traced by EMS World

00:05:43.057 --> 00:05:46.237
to economist Jack Stout and the University of Oklahoma team

00:05:46.237 --> 00:05:49.577
in the late 1970s separate clinical oversight

00:05:49.577 --> 00:05:51.577
from operations through competitive bidding

00:05:51.577 --> 00:05:53.017
and performance contracts.

00:05:53.017 --> 00:05:58.217
Under such arrangements, the authority owns the assets and the receivables while contractors

00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:00.557
compete on cost and on response metrics.

00:06:01.257 --> 00:06:06.177
A redesign would first separate the fixed cost of coverage from per-ride billing, funding

00:06:06.177 --> 00:06:08.637
stand by directly through contracts or taxes.

00:06:09.297 --> 00:06:14.497
It would next expand community-paramedicent to handle lower acuity calls on site, lowering

00:06:14.497 --> 00:06:17.017
the number of full ambulance dispatches required.

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:22.417
Scale would then allow fewer total units to cover the same geography once response patterns

00:06:22.417 --> 00:06:27.477
are measured rather than assumed. The hard constraint remains guaranteeing response times

00:06:27.477 --> 00:06:32.617
during peak demand and night shifts. Any model must still solve that coverage problem without

00:06:32.617 --> 00:06:37.717
inflating the price attached to each transport that does occur. Even with reduced overall

00:06:37.717 --> 00:06:43.037
dispatches, peak hour surges and rural coverage gaps would still require some minimum number

00:06:43.037 --> 00:06:47.937
of staffed units, and that daily cost must be met through dedicated payments rather than

00:06:47.937 --> 00:06:52.977
ride-by-ride charges. The first concrete signal of progress would be a published contract

00:06:52.977 --> 00:06:57.737
that pays an operator a fixed daily rate for coverage, and a separate, much smaller

00:06:57.737 --> 00:07:03.437
fee only for actual transports performed. Readiness is a fixed daily purchase whose costs

00:07:03.437 --> 00:07:07.497
cannot be wished away by billing it only to the patients who happened to need transport

00:07:07.497 --> 00:07:12.657
that day. The crew was already on the clock before anyone called, and the crew remains

00:07:12.657 --> 00:07:17.917
on the clock after the call is done. A quiet shift does not refund the day, handing

00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:21.977
Getting the whole daily sum to whichever rides happen to be billable does not change what

00:07:21.977 --> 00:07:22.837
the community bought.

00:07:23.357 --> 00:07:27.657
It only chooses who has handed the invoice, and it makes the charge look like a price

00:07:27.657 --> 00:07:30.817
for motion when the thing being sold was a day of being available.

00:07:31.557 --> 00:07:36.017
Separating coverage contracts from transport fees lets a community buy the first service

00:07:36.017 --> 00:07:39.657
at its actual scale and the second at its marginal cost.

00:07:40.217 --> 00:07:45.317
The coverage contract is the promise, staff time held to a response standard, including

00:07:45.317 --> 00:07:50.937
the hours when almost nobody calls. That promise has a size you can write down as a day of coverage,

00:07:51.417 --> 00:07:56.817
and you pay for the day whether the radio stays silent or not. The transport fee is the other purchase,

00:07:57.217 --> 00:08:02.237
fuel and wear, and the clinical work of a trip that actually happened. Those two purchases do

00:08:02.237 --> 00:08:07.597
not belong on the same line, because one stays put when volume falls, and the other moves only

00:08:07.597 --> 00:08:13.357
when a van rolls. The same separation applies whenever a necessary standby function is financed

00:08:13.357 --> 00:08:16.497
through usage charges instead of dedicated capacity payments.

00:08:17.037 --> 00:08:21.757
If the thing you need is someone waiting, pricing only the moments of use loads the weight

00:08:21.757 --> 00:08:23.757
onto whoever happens to trigger the meter.

00:08:24.417 --> 00:08:28.277
A from scratch look at a bill like this does not start with the steal in the van.

00:08:28.877 --> 00:08:32.457
It starts by sorting capacity you must hold from a trip you can count.

00:08:33.057 --> 00:08:37.537
Who will first publish the measured cost of a full day of ambulance coverage under a direct

00:08:37.537 --> 00:08:38.577
readiness contract?

00:08:38.577 --> 00:08:43.797
and what utilization threshold would make that contract cheaper than today's per-ride model.

00:08:44.277 --> 00:08:48.917
That's First Principles Daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:08:49.437 --> 00:08:52.657
share it with someone who'd appreciate it and subscribe wherever you listen.

00:08:53.217 --> 00:08:57.697
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example or one opportunity every day.

